  translation: "1. Arjuna said: Some devotees, constantly engaged in this way, worship you; others worship the Imperishable, the unmanifest. Which of these know yoga best?\n2. The Blessed Lord said: Those who fix the mind on me and worship me, ever steadfast and endowed with supreme faith, I hold to be the best yogins.\n3. But those who contemplate the Imperishable, which is indefinable, unmanifest, all-pervading, unthinkable, unchanging, immovable and eternal,\n4. having fully restrained all the senses, even-minded at all times, delighting in the good of all beings: they reach me alone.\n5. Greater is the trouble of those whose minds are set on the unmanifest, for the goal that is the unmanifest is reached with difficulty by those who identify with the body.\n6. But those who give up all actions to me, hold me as the supreme, and worship me, meditating on me with a concentration that has no other object,\n7. for them, whose minds are fixed on me, O Pārtha, I soon become the deliverer from the ocean of mortal saṃsāra.\n8. Fix the mind on me alone; settle the intellect in me. You will dwell in me alone hereafter. Of this there is no doubt.\n9. If you cannot fix the mind steadily on me, then seek to reach me by the yoga of practice, O Dhanaṃjaya.\n10. If you are unable even to practise, be intent on work for me. Even by doing actions for my sake you will attain perfection.\n11. If you are unable to do even this, then, relying on my yoga and self-controlled, give up the fruit of all actions.\n12. Knowledge is indeed better than practice; meditation is superior to knowledge; giving up the fruit of action is superior to meditation. From that giving up, peace follows at once.\n13. He who hates no being, who is friendly and compassionate, free from 'mine' and 'I', the same in pain and pleasure, forbearing,\n14. always content, a yogin, self-controlled, firm in conviction, with mind and intellect given over to me: he, my devotee, is dear to me.\n15. He from whom the world does not shrink, and who does not shrink from the world, who is free from elation, intolerance, fear and agitation: he too is dear to me.\n16. He who wants nothing, who is pure, capable, impartial and free from fear, who has given up every undertaking: he, my devotee, is dear to me.\n17. He who neither rejoices nor hates, neither grieves nor longs, who has given up good and bad actions and is full of devotion: he is dear to me.\n18. The same toward enemy and friend, and likewise in honour and dishonour, the same in cold and heat, pleasure and pain, free from attachment,\n19. to whom blame and praise are alike, silent, content with whatever comes, homeless, steady in understanding, full of devotion: that person is dear to me.\n20. But those who follow this nectar of dharma as it has been taught, with faith, holding me as the highest goal: those devotees are exceedingly dear to me.",
